# GATE FOUR: THE EMPTY HOUSE

> SUPER: 2030

.INT. EVAN'S APARTMENT - NIGHT

A one-bedroom apartment above a dentist's office.

Not depressing. Worse: temporary-looking after eighteen months.

Evan eats cereal at a small table while an AI-generated documentary plays on the wall.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The reconstruction shown here is probabilistic. No direct visual record exists of this event.

On screen, mammoths move across Pleistocene Ohio with the calm authority of footage shot yesterday.

Evan pauses it.

He looks out his real window at a parking lot.

Unpauses.

.INT. REGIONAL MUTUAL - IT FLOOR - DAY

The old IT floor is now a shared “human escalation space.”

Evan and Julian are two of six people where there used to be forty.

A ceiling display shows cases mostly resolved by autonomous systems.

JULIAN
You ever miss printers?

EVAN
No.

JULIAN
Me neither. Just checking if nostalgia had fully destroyed you.

EVAN
I miss knowing what my job was.

JULIAN
Our job is to be blamed by both sides when the machine and the person disagree.

EVAN
So IT.

JULIAN
Exactly.

Julian tosses him a small package.

EVAN
What's this?

JULIAN
Housewarming.

EVAN
I've lived there a year and a half.

JULIAN
And yet it still looks like you are hiding from a landlord.

Evan opens it.

A framed print:

MAKE THE WEEK FEEL BIGGER.

EVAN
You remembered that?

JULIAN
No. Your old assistant remembered it. You apparently said it forty-seven times between 2023 and 2027.

Evan looks at the frame.

EVAN
That's creepy.

JULIAN
You opted into memory.

EVAN
Still creepy.

JULIAN
Correct.

.INT. MARA'S COLLEGE AUDITORIUM - DAY

Graduation.

Evan and Claire sit beside each other like people who have learned the choreography of being exes.

Mara crosses the stage.

They both stand too fast.

EVAN
That's my kid!

CLAIRE
She knows.

EVAN
I wasn't telling her.

Afterward--

.EXT. CAMPUS QUAD - DAY

Photos. Flowers. Heat.

Mara wears a graduation robe over sneakers.

MARA
One normal picture. Please.

EVAN
I am normal.

CLAIRE
This is going well.

They pose.

A stranger takes the photo.

STRANGER
Got it.

The phone immediately offers:

REMOVE BACKGROUND PEOPLE?
IMPROVE LIGHTING?
OPEN EYES?
GENERATE BEST EXPRESSION?

Mara taps NO TO ALL.

MARA
I want the one that happened.

Evan notices the phrase.

CLAIRE notices him noticing.

CLAIRE
Don't.

EVAN
I didn't say anything.

CLAIRE
Your eyebrows did.

Evan looks at her.

EVAN
Julian says that.

CLAIRE
Julian is observant.

Mara groans.

MARA
Can my graduation not become evidence in whatever this is.

EVAN
Whatever what is?

MARA
Exactly.

.INT. EVAN'S APARTMENT - LATE NIGHT

Evan opens a storage box labeled MARA / HOME VIDEO.

Inside: old mini-DV tapes, memory cards, a broken camcorder.

He loads digitized clips.

Mara at two.

Mara at six.

Mara at eleven, refusing to be filmed.

Ray in the background of a Christmas video, alive and completely unimportant to the scene at the time.

Evan freezes him.

Ray is carrying a folding chair through the frame.

For twelve seconds, the camera happened to preserve a dead man doing nothing.

Evan zooms.

The software offers:

RECONSTRUCT FACE DETAIL?

He almost selects it.

Instead closes the menu.

INT. PUBLIC LIBRARY - EVENING

A small lecture advertised as:

MEMORY IS NOT A VIDEO: WHAT NEUROSCIENCE ACTUALLY KNOWS

DR. LEENA SHAH, 50s, speaks to perhaps sixty people.

No giant stage. No mysticism.

A slide shows a stylized network of neurons.

DR. SHAH
People ask where a memory is stored. That's a useful question right up until the word “where” starts misleading you.

Evan, back row, takes notes.

DR. SHAH (CONT'D)
A memory is not a tiny picture hidden inside the head. Learning changes biological systems. Synapses change. Excitability changes. Networks become easier to re-enter. Later, a partial cue can drive enough of that system back toward a learned state that the organism behaves as though the past is present again.

A hand goes up.

AUDIENCE MEMBER
So like a computer file?

DR. SHAH
Only if you imagine a computer file that changes the computer by being written, is reconstructed differently every time you open it, and can alter itself because you opened it.

Laughter.

Evan underlines that.

DR. SHAH (CONT'D)
The analogy isn't useless. It's just expensive. Every analogy imports assumptions.

Evan writes:

MEMORY = CHANGE THAT MAKES RETURN EASIER?

Then beneath it:

WEIGHTS?

He circles it.

.INT. LIBRARY HALLWAY - LATER

People queue to speak to Dr. Shah.

Evan considers joining.

He sees someone ahead asking a long question while everyone behind them suffers politely.

He leaves.

.INT. EVAN'S APARTMENT - NIGHT

A research assistant model is open across three displays.

EVAN
Explain an engram without using storage metaphors.

AI
An engram is a persistent physical alteration in a biological system caused by experience, such that later activity can partially reinstate consequences of that experience.

EVAN
“Persistent physical alteration” sounds like weights.

AI
There is a loose analogy to learned parameters in artificial networks, but it can mislead. Biological memory also involves ongoing dynamics, neuromodulation, structural change, gene expression, systems consolidation--

EVAN
Okay. What carries the load?

AI
Can you clarify which load?

Evan stops.

His father's phrase has escaped into a machine.

EVAN
The information.

AI
There may be no single carrier. Different aspects of a memory can depend on distributed changes across multiple scales.

EVAN
Then what is the memory when nobody is remembering it?

The answer takes a little longer.

AI
Not an experience waiting in darkness. A changed system with altered possibilities.

Evan leans back.

He writes that down.

“ALTERED POSSIBILITIES.”

.INT. GROCERY STORE - DAY

Evan walks with smart glasses off, deliberately using his own eyes.

A CHILD ahead drops an orange.

It rolls.

For one instant, because of shape and motion, Evan sees the red rubber ball from 2004.

Not hallucination.

Association.

The child retrieves the orange.

Evan watches the mother wipe it with her shirt and put it back in the cart.

His brain has reconstructed twenty-six years in less than a second.

.INT. EVAN'S APARTMENT - NIGHT

He calls Mara.

MARA appears on screen in a classroom, hanging student artwork.

MARA
You know I'm still at work.

EVAN
I have a question.

MARA
Of course you do.

EVAN
When you remember Grandpa, what's the first thing?

MARA thinks.

MARA
His hands.

EVAN
Why?

MARA
I don't know. They were huge.

EVAN
Mine aren't small.

MARA
Yours are computer hands.

EVAN
What does that mean?

MARA
Soft.

EVAN
Rude.

MARA
He had cuts everywhere. And that black stuff around his nails that never came out.

EVAN
Grease.

MARA
I know it was grease.

EVAN
No, I just--

He stops himself.

MARA
Why are you asking?

EVAN
I went to this talk about memory.

MARA
Oh no.

EVAN
It was good.

MARA
Dad.

EVAN
What.

MARA
You can miss Grandpa without inventing a field of study around it.

He smiles.

EVAN
Apparently the field already exists.

MARA
Very convenient.

Behind her, a child-painted paper sun hangs crooked.

EVAN
You like teaching?

MARA
Most days.

EVAN
Good.

MARA
That wasn't your question.

EVAN
No.

MARA
Maybe it should be sometimes.

That lands without cruelty.

EVAN
Okay.

MARA
Okay.

They stay connected another moment with nothing to say.

Neither hangs up.

.INT. EVAN'S APARTMENT - LATER

Evan opens a new notebook.

On the first page:

WHAT CARRIES THE LOAD?

Under it:

BRAIN
MODEL
MEMORY
WORLD?

He stares at the last word.

Crosses it out.

Then, after a beat, writes it again smaller.

> END GATE FOUR.
